      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The Heart Protection Study (HPS) results were presented shortly after the National Cholesterol Education Program released the third report of clinical practice guidelines for the evaluation and management of elevated cholesterol in adults. Both the guidelines and the HPS results support an aggressive approach to managing high-risk individuals. The HPS showed that cardiovascular events and mortality were reduced in high-risk patients taking simvastatin regardless of baseline low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol levels. Some of these patients would not have met criteria for drug treatment according to the guidelines. The conclusion was that all high-risk patients with LDL > or = 100 mg/dL should be treated with drug therapy with the goal of reducing LDL to < 100 mg/dL."},
